## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates practical design skills for privacy-aware, scalable data ingestion, testing strategies, and architectural decision-making under regional compliance requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Centralized policy registry with dynamic privacy rule enforcement
- Per-record lineage tracking via unique identifiers
- Iceberg upserts with schema evolution support
- Regional isolation and late-data handling (up to 60 minutes)
- Privacy conformance tests and data-quality validation gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Policy application sketch
function applyPolicy(record, policyRegistry) {
  const policies = policyRegistry.getPolicies(record.region, record.dataType);
  return policies.reduce((r, policy) => policy.transform(r), record);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Design a scalable, auditable CDC ingestion that evolves schemas safely while preserving privacy and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Central schema registry with versioned contracts
- Idempotent upserts and MERGE semantics
- Dynamic schema evolution and tenant isolation
- Late data buffering and privacy-preserving BI views
- End-to-end testing and monitoring of contracts and lineage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: contract validation and upsert
function upsertEvent(event, registry) {
  const contract = registry.getContract(event.version, event.schemaKey);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  // perform idempotent merge into Iceberg
  mergeIntoIceberg(event, contract.primaryKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift in production and implement safe rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant data isolation and lineage end-to-end?
